;; schedule-fixed's copybook, read the other way: the table slides, so the item
;; behind it moves with the count and the record's extent is data rather than a
;; constant. A fixed-length dataset could not carry such a record at all, which
;; is why this one is framed, and the record descriptor word is the whole of the
;; difference the framing makes to the bytes.
;;
;; The schedule is the same text as schedule-fixed's, and that is the claim.
;; Which reading applies is a property of the extract rather than of the
;; copybook, and it must not also decide whether the copybook can be described:
;; a schedule is available under both readings, and the descriptors differ in
;; the table's repetition and in nothing about the variant.
;;
;; What the count does here is decide how many of the scheduled arms are taken.
;; The second record holds one entry, so only the arm scheduled for occurrence
;; one is reached and the record carries no slack run at all; the third holds
;; three, so two of them are work entries and the record carries two. Which
;; runs a record has is therefore the count's, while which occurrence takes
;; which arm stays the descriptor's.
(encoding
  (charset ascii)
  (sign-convention ascii-zone-37)
  (byte-order big-endian)
  (float-format ieee-754))

(framing
  (recfm VB))

(record ADDRESS
  (copybook "address.cpy" ADR-RECORD))

(copybook-reading
  (occurs-depending-on odoslide))

(discriminate ADDRESS (equals (item ADDRESS ADR-TYPE) "A"))

(schedule-variant (item ADDRESS ADR-ENTRY ADR-HOME)
  (arm ADR-HOME 1)
  (arm ADR-WORK 2 3)
  (arm ADR-MAIL 4))

(sequence (* ADDRESS))
